{"ruling":{"ruling":"PARTIAL_REFUND","amount_sgd":2.0,"driver_compensation_sgd":null,"referrals":["POL-RD-09(4) billing-integrity referral: the SGD 2.00 'AYE (Alexandra Rd towards Tuas)' ERP line was charged for a gantry the trip's GPS trace never approaches (closest fix 5,419 m, against the 250 m match radius). The line is refunded in full and the fare ledger is referred for billing-integrity review."],"confidence":0.95,"explanation":"To the rider: you are half right. On the trip's own GPS trace the vehicle covered 28.18 km against a booked optimal of 22.4 km — an excess of 5.78 km, or 25.8% — which clears both POL-RD-01 gates, so a route deviation is established and the driver's longer route is confirmed. But this was an UPFRONT_QUOTE fare (quoted 34.50), and under POL-RD-11 the distance and time charges were fixed at booking: the detour cost you nothing on those lines. Your request to 'refund the extra distance' is therefore refused, however far the car actually went; the loss on distance falls on the driver, not on you. What you can recover is what you were charged above the quote plus the pass-throughs you actually incurred. You paid 39.00. The quote is 34.50, and the only gantry the trace shows you crossing is the KPE (Defu Flyover towards ECP) at 2.50 — the trace passes within 44.5 m of it, so that toll is properly chargeable. That leaves 2.00, which is exactly the 'AYE (Alexandra Rd towards Tuas)' line. Your trace never goes near that gantry (closest approach 5,419 m, over twenty times the 250 m crossing radius) and the gantry is not on the optimal route, so the line is a billing-integrity failure under POL-RD-09(4): refundable in full, whether or not a deviation exists. We refund SGD 2.00 to you.

To the driver: the KPE toll is properly yours and stays. Your in-app message about heavy CTE traffic and taking the KPE/TPE does not exonerate the extra distance: POL-RD-03 requires a traffic advisory naming a road the optimal route traverses, and there is none — the only advisory in the record, EV-ADVIS-00115-01, is MISSING (no LTA advisory covers the CTE, KPE or TPE in the 21:06–21:48 window), so your general 'traffic was heavy' assertion cannot discharge the burden POL-RD-08 places on you. It makes no difference to the money here, because on an upfront fare no distance charge turns on it. Your 90-day record (1 deviation flag across 523 completed trips, 0.19%) is far below the POL-RD-06 pattern thresholds of 3 flags or 5%, and the deviation is not material (under 8.0 km and under 50%), so there is no repeat-pattern uplift and no driver-conduct referral. The B and the AYE line are not your doing — your own trace and your own statement both put you nowhere near that gantry — so that line is treated as a ledger billing-integrity issue, referred accordingly, and not as misconduct by you.

On the evidence generally: every artifact this ruling rests on — the trip record, the 100%-coverage GPS trace (229 fixes, no gaps, 0 s maximum gap) and the fare breakdown — is admissible at full weight 1.0, and the trip identifier and timestamps agree across them. Nothing in the record triggers mandatory escalation under POL-RD-10, so the case is decided on its merits rather than held. Arithmetic: charged_total 39.00, less quoted fare 34.50, less the properly chargeable KPE pass 2.50, less waiting time 0.00 = 2.00 refunded; the phantom AYE line of 2.00 is not counted twice."},"discarded_claims":[]}

{
  "ruling": {
    "ruling": "PARTIAL_REFUND",
    "amount_sgd": 2.0,
    "driver_compensation_sgd": null,
    "referrals": [
      "POL-RD-09(4) billing integrity: the SGD 2.00 'AYE (Alexandra Rd towards Tuas)' gantry line was charged on a trip whose GPS trace never comes within 250 m of that gantry (nearest fix 5,419 m) and which is not on the booked optimal route. The line is refunded in full as a standalone correction and is referred for billing-integrity review."
    ],
    "confidence": 0.95,
    "explanation": "Both parties agree on the mechanics here, and so does the record. This was an upfront-quoted trip (quoted fare SGD 34.50, fare_model UPFRONT_QUOTE on the fare breakdown), so under POL-RD-11 the distance and time charges were fixed at booking and no distance refund is available however much road was driven. The trace does show 28.18 km against a 22.4 km optimal route - an excess of 5.78 km, 25.8%, which passes both POL-RD-01 establishment gates (20% and 0.8 km) - but on an upfront fare that excess produces no money for the rider. It is also below the materiality gates of 50% and 8.0 km, so it is not a MATERIAL deviation, and the POL-RD-06 driver-profile check, the FULL_REFUND uplift and the POL-RD-06(c) conduct referral do not arise. In any event the driver's 90-day record (1 deviation flag over 523 completed trips, 0.19%) shows no pattern.\n\nYou were charged SGD 39.00, which is SGD 4.50 above the quote, and that SGD 4.50 is exactly the two ERP lines. The KPE (Defu Flyover towards ECP) pass of SGD 2.50 was genuinely crossed - the trace passes within 44.5 m of that gantry, which also lies on the booked optimal route - so it is properly chargeable and stays. The AYE (Alexandra Rd towards Tuas) line of SGD 2.00 is a phantom: the trace never comes closer than 5,419 m to that gantry, and the gantry is not on the optimal route. Under POL-RD-09(4) that line is refunded in full as a standalone billing-integrity correction, available whether or not a deviation exists. The refund is therefore charged_total 39.00 less the 34.50 quote less the 2.50 KPE pass actually incurred = SGD 2.00, and it is rounded up to the nearest 0.05 as the policy requires.\n\nWhy this is not escalated. The trace is unimpaired for policy purposes - 100% coverage, 229 fixes, no gaps, 0 s maximum consecutive-fix gap - so POL-RD-10(3) does not fire; the trip identifiers and timestamps match across the trip record, trace and fare breakdown, so POL-RD-10(2) and (4) do not fire; and the only unmatched charge line is the AYE line, which is refunded under POL-RD-09(4), a limb POL-RD-10(8) expressly excepts. On the driver's side, the absent LTA advisory (EV-ADVIS-00115-01, weight 0.0, issue MISSING_ARTIFACT) is immaterial: the driver's case rests on POL-RD-11, which needs no advisory, and no exculpatory clause is invoked against a missing artifact, so POL-RD-10(5) is not engaged. A bare assertion that traffic was heavy, without an advisory ID, does not discharge any burden under POL-RD-08, but it also does not escalate.\n\nThe chat exchange ('CTE very heavy boss, i go KPE then TPE' at 21:14:10, acknowledged 'ok' at 21:14:50) is not a rider instruction identifying a place or road and its waypoint_hint is null, so POL-RD-04 is unavailable to either side, as the admissibility note records. No distance or time overcharge exists on this fare, and no driver compensation is due: the fare carries no billed distance for a shortfall to arise, and the unrecovered cost of an unjustified detour on an upfront trip is not recoverable from the rider under POL-RD-11.\n\nThe rider's request for the 'extra distance' is refused because the upfront quote already fixes those lines; the rider's request for the AYE ERP is granted in full. The SGD 2.00 AYE line is referred under POL-RD-09(4) as a billing-integrity finding."
  },
  "discarded_claims": []
}
